        "type": "application",
        "title": "Reading NLP in the Wild — and Its Limits",
        "content": "Understanding how NLP works lets you use these tools wisely and recognize their failure modes — a crucial literacy in an age of AI.\n\nWHERE IT SHINES: Machine translation now handles dozens of language pairs at near-professional quality for common languages. Search engines interpret intent, not just keywords. Voice assistants transcribe speech in real time. Autocomplete and grammar tools speed up writing. Sentiment analysis lets companies scan millions of reviews. Large language models draft emails, summarize documents, and answer questions across virtually every domain.\n\nWHERE IT BREAKS: Because LLMs predict plausible text rather than retrieve verified facts, they can HALLUCINATE — stating false things fluently and confidently. They inherit BIASES from their training data, reproducing stereotypes present in human text. They struggle with LOW-RESOURCE languages that lack large digital corpora, deepening a digital divide. They have no genuine grounding in the physical world, so they can fail at simple reasoning a child would find obvious. And their fluency can mislead: sounding confident is not the same as being correct.\n\nHOW TO USE THIS: Treat an LLM as a brilliant, fast, but sometimes unreliable assistant. VERIFY factual claims against primary sources. Be skeptical when a system is confident about specifics — names, numbers, citations. Remember that quality degrades for less common languages and specialized domains. And appreciate the deep linguistic point: these systems learned language from statistics alone, with no innate grammar and no experience of the world, yet they capture syntax, semantics, and much of pragmatics. Whether that means they truly 'understand' language, or merely simulate it convincingly, is one of the great open questions where linguistics, computer science, and philosophy now meet.",
